s that we know  it will be kind of offensive to some but  it won't be offensive to everybody and  those it's not it'll be like a magnet  and even if it's one person I think  that's a better way to to do your career  than to join the kind of endless sea of  generic faceless personality list music  that exists out  there because a lot of that I think is  written from a place of fear and so try  to get over your fear and just be you  which I know is easier said than done  but all the composers I admire most  that's the one thing they all seem to  have in common the criteria for a new  project is its threefold but it's really  primarily between the first two which is  who am i working with so it doesn't  matter to me what the game is in terms  of genre medium console release that  kind of thing know that matters to me  compared to who it is there are certain  people that I would just I would go over  a cliff for them because we've been in  the trenches together you know I mean  that game company is a great example  where we did flow and then of course  this incredible experience together on  journey he was life-changing both during  and afterward and so it's for them they  say jump I say how high that kind of  thing that matters to me far more than  any other and so most the times and I  work with someone it's immediately about  the chemistry of the collaborators  because you bleed together that that's  the thing it's so deeply collaborative  there's so much about the openness of  their ideas and their openness to my  idea is that that matters the most and  then the second criteria would be the  musical opportunity is it give me a  chance to do something I never done  before now if it's somebody I've worked  with and I love them that becomes less  important to me but if it's a new  relationship then that's the first thing  I look to what can I do that feels fresh  then only once you've got all that  figured out do I start thinking about  the basics of how does it pay wh