ut  craft he was in this incredible artist  so as a result he was deeply chameleon  ik his music ran every genre you could  possibly imagine and yet when you study  his work you realize below that surface  of genre conventions and choices of  instruments and that kind of thing  there's a very consistent voice as an  artist that's very strong I mean you can  spot Jerry's music once you know it  within five seconds that's unmistakably  Goldsmith and that to me became the  model I loved the idea that genre is  actually superficial and I liken it to  the clothes that I wear like today I'm  wearing this tie and a black shirt and I  look you know border I'm threatening to  look posh  if I dare borrow British language but  other days it's just a t-shirt that's 15  years past when it should have been  thrown out and tennis shoes that have  holes in them and that kind of thing and  the way I see it is that's genre the  clothes I'm wearing are not the same  thing is the person wearing the clothes  and so to me the idea of being diverse  or  sort of chameleon in one's work is is  ultimately addressing the surface of  what they're doing it's not who they are  on a deeper level there are very few of  my kind of film heroes still around but  Peter McConnell for example is one of my  absolute all-time favorite composers he  did all of the LucasArts titles of the  90s and the  which is for me my other favorite game  scorer of all time grim fandago and he  continues to be incredibly relevant in  the industry you know very one of the  very few composers who got their start  in the 80s and early 90s are still part  of the actual forefront of the  conversation today I mean that's just  such an enduring career it's hard in any  medium to to last that long but you know  he's the composer of hearthstone and and  all the current you know Psychonauts 2  which is forthcoming and everyone's  always excited what Double Fine's up to  and etcetera etcetera etc and so the  fact that I get to call